+# Security audit and deployment
+
+This audit covers the repository's Node HTTP server and static coffee shop page.
+It does not verify a live deployment, CDN configuration, or browser behavior.
+Run `npm test` for automated HTTP and regression checks; `npm run build` checks
+server syntax. No browser verification was performed.
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+| Finding | Implemented protection |
+| --- | --- |
+| No security response headers | Central headers apply to pages, CSS, errors, logout, and reporting responses. |
+| Inline stylesheet and style attribute | Moved styling to `/styles.css` and a class so CSP needs no inline-style exception. |
+| No injection defenses | Enforced CSP denies resources by default, blocks all scripts, objects, frames, inline styles, and base tags, and restricts forms to this origin. Trusted Types checks are required and policy creation is disabled. |
+| Clickjacking and cross-origin exposure | Same-origin framing, CORP, COOP `same-origin-allow-popups`, Fetch Metadata restrictions, and cache variation. Same-site subdomains are not implicitly trusted. |
+| No rollout telemetry | CSP `report-to` and legacy `report-uri` target `/security-reports`; both enforcement and trial modes retain reporting. |
+| Unbounded or sensitive telemetry risk | Collector limits body size (16 KiB), batch size (20), and request volume (60/minute per process). Logs retain only allowlisted report type, effective directive, and disposition. |
+| Logout reachable via navigation/prefetch | GET and HEAD now show a confirmation without changing state. POST requires an exact configured Origin, checks Fetch Metadata, sends `Clear-Site-Data`, and disables caching. |
+| Unvalidated request/configuration assumptions | Uses a fixed URL parser base, explicit route list, method restrictions, correct MIME types, and validated origin/policy/HSTS settings. Never trusts Host or forwarding headers for security decisions. |
+
+The page has no JavaScript, DOM sinks, postMessage, third-party scripts, webhooks,
+SSO, payments, embeds, cookies, or actual authentication/session backend. Existing
+navigation routes continue to serve the same static page. Logout clears browser
+site data where supported; a future authentication implementation must also revoke
+the server session and expire its cookies. Use `__Host-` session cookies with
+Secure, HttpOnly, Path=/, no Domain, and SameSite=Lax when introducing authentication.
+
+The only external dependency is the existing HTTPS Unsplash background image.
+Only `https://images.unsplash.com` is allowed for external images. There are no
+scripts needing nonces, hashes, or SRI. If scripts are introduced, audit them and
+use nonce/hash authorization before changing the script policy. Unsupported
+Trusted Types browsers still receive the CSP script prohibition.
+
+Camera, microphone, location, payment, and USB features are disabled. No CORS
+permissions are granted. COEP/document isolation are omitted because the app does
+not use shared memory and enforcing them could disrupt external assets.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Requires a supported Node.js release (automated checks run with Node 24).
+There are no runtime dependencies.
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `PORT` | `11222` | HTTP listener port. |
+| `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` | `http://localhost:PORT` | Exact browser-facing origin, including nonstandard port. Required as HTTPS in production. Used for report URLs and logout Origin checks. No path, credentials, query, or fragment. |
+| `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE` | `enforce` | `enforce` or `report-only`; invalid values fail startup. |
+| `HSTS_MAX_AGE` | `300` | HTTPS HSTS lifetime in seconds, from 0 to 31536000. No subdomain or preload commitment. |
+
+Local development:
+
+```sh
+npm start
+```
+
+Open the app using the configured origin. For a preview hostname or a LAN IP,
+set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to that URL so logout Origin checks match.
+
+Production rollout example (replace the example origin):
+
+```sh
+NODE_ENV=production PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://coffee.example SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=report-only npm start
+```
+
+1. Put the HTTP listener behind a trusted TLS reverse proxy. Restrict direct
+   origin access, redirect public HTTP to HTTPS at the edge, and preserve the
+   application headers without adding conflicting policies. Set body-size,
+   connection, request-rate, and log-volume limits at the edge as well.
+2. Deploy `report-only` for an existing deployment with unknown integrations.
+   CSP and COOP candidates generate reports; framing/object/base restrictions and
+   companion headers remain enforced. The audited static app defaults to full
+   enforcement. Fetch Metadata restrictions remain active in both modes: test
+   any new cross-site API, webhook, or SSO endpoint before adding it.
+3. Collect telemetry over representative traffic for days to weeks. Reports are
+   emitted as JSON to stdout; route these events to your existing log pipeline.
+   Group by directive and disposition. Treat all reports as spoofable hints,
+   distinguish extensions/crawler noise from app regressions, and never broaden
+   the policy automatically based on submitted reports.
+4. Promote with `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=enforce` after investigating legitimate
+   violations. Keep reporting enabled, monitor errors, and use `npm test` in CI
+   to guard changes to routes, headers, styles, and external dependencies.
+5. If policy regressions occur, restore the previous release or temporarily use
+   `report-only` while fixing the incompatibility. HSTS remains independent:
+   start at 300 seconds, increase only after HTTPS is stable, and use
+   `HSTS_MAX_AGE=0` over HTTPS to remove the cached policy. Do not add preload or
+   includeSubDomains until every affected hostname is ready.
+
+Modern reporting endpoints need HTTPS; local HTTP tests verify endpoint wiring
+and ingestion, not browser report delivery. `report-uri` remains as a fallback.
+Reporting is best effort, and the process-wide collector limit can drop telemetry
+during spikes. Multi-instance deployments need shared edge limits. The collector
+does not log raw URLs, paths, queries, samples, IPs, or user agents. Reports may
+contain those fields on ingress: disable raw request/body logging at the proxy
+and collector, apply any required edge redaction before storage, and define
+retention/access rules for your log pipeline. This intentionally minimal log
+schema sacrifices resource-level diagnostics to avoid storing sensitive data.
+
+`Clear-Site-Data` is intended for HTTPS and may affect cookies shared across
+subdomains. Review that scope when adding other applications to the deployment.
+
+References: [MDN CSP deployment and reporting](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP),
+[Reporting-Endpoints](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Reporting-Endpoints),
+and [Clear-Site-Data](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Clear-Site-Data).
